# Rodeo environment variables.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Local development needs DATABASE_URL and ALLOW_DEV_AUTH: auth falls back to the
# yopmail bypass and email is printed to the console. Everything below the first
# section is required on the production deploy and validated at boot by
# src/lib/server/env.ts.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Always required
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Local Postgres (see docker-compose.yml). On Vercel this is the Supabase
# *transaction pooler* URL (port 6543) — the app queries through it at runtime.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://rodeo:rodeo@localhost:5432/rodeo-development"

# What the Prisma CLI (migrate, db seed, studio) connects through: a direct or
# *session pooler* (port 5432) connection, because migrations cannot run through
# the transaction pooler. Optional locally — prisma.config.ts falls back to
# DATABASE_URL, which is already a direct connection in local dev.
# DIRECT_URL=

# Per-instance pg pool cap (defaults to 3 in src/lib/server/db.ts). Matters on
# serverless, where each warm instance holds its own pool. Leave unset locally.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=3

# Set by CI on preview deploys only: the per-branch Postgres schema this deploy
# lives in (Supabase's pooler only serves the default database, so preview
# isolation is by schema). Unset means `public` — production and local dev.
# DATABASE_SCHEMA=

# The second factor on the two whole-database operations in Settings: "Export
# all data", which produces one JSON file containing every applicant's name,
# email and answers, and "Export and wipe database", which then empties every
# table except the staff accounts and the blacklist.
#
# Being a signed-in admin is not enough for either. This is a shared secret held
# outside the app, so a hijacked admin session cannot exfiltrate or destroy the
# database on its own — the same reason a bank asks again before a wire.
#
# Unset means both operations refuse, and that is the intended default. Set it
# only where those buttons should work, and do not reuse a password from
# anywhere else.
# ADMIN_DATA_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local development only
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Turns on the yopmail bypass: /login signs in any @yopmail.com account in the
# database with no password at all. Never set this anywhere reachable from the
# internet — `npm run db:seed` creates admin1@yopmail.com as a full admin.
#
# It exists because the bypass used to switch itself on whenever VERCEL_ENV was
# unset and NODE_ENV wasn't 'production', which meant a self-hosted deploy that
# forgot one variable served passwordless admin logins. The app now refuses to
# boot in that state unless this is explicitly set to 1 — AND DATABASE_URL points
# at a local database (see src/lib/server/env.ts).
#
# Left commented so it is never enabled just by copying this file. Uncomment it for
# local development; the app prints a clear boot error telling you to if you forget.

# ALLOW_DEV_AUTH=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Auth — required in production, unused locally
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Production signs in through Supabase — Google OAuth or email/password — and
# the first login creates the User row. Enable the Google and Email providers
# in the Supabase dashboard and register
# https://<your-domain>/auth/callback as an authorised redirect URL (email
# confirmation links land there too).
#
# The anon key is a public credential — it is served to the browser by design.

SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email — one transport required in production
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Resend is the only transport, and production refuses to boot without the key.
# Generate one in the Resend dashboard under API Keys.
#
# There used to be a nodemailer SMTP fallback here (EMAIL_HOST / EMAIL_PORT /
# EMAIL_USER / EMAIL_PASS). It is gone. Resend took precedence whenever the key
# was set — which is always, in every deployed environment — so the fallback was
# never once exercised, and the Amazon SES credentials behind it had long since
# gone stale. If you find those variables still set in Vercel, delete them.
#
# Production is the only environment that sends. Development and preview both
# print the message instead, whether or not a key is present — so this variable
# is only needed on production.

RESEND_API_KEY=

# From: address on everything the app sends. Resend rejects the message unless
# this domain is verified in the same Resend account.
EMAIL_FROM=hello@freetailhackers.com

# The canonical public URL, used for links that leave the app — the wallet
# buttons in the {{check_in_pass}} email block point here.
#
# Not derived from the incoming request on purpose: an admin sending from a
# preview deployment would otherwise mail every recipient links back to that
# preview host. On Vercel this falls back to VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL (and
# VERCEL_URL on a preview), so a deploy that forgets it still produces working
# links — but set it explicitly in production.

APP_ORIGIN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Wallet passes — optional everywhere
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Apple Wallet and Google Wallet versions of the check-in QR code, offered on
# /hacker/account to confirmed attendees once the RSVP deadline has passed.
#
# Nothing in this section is validated at boot, on purpose. A provider with no
# variables set is switched off and its button never renders; the site is
# otherwise unaffected. Half-configured providers print a warning at startup
# naming the variables they are missing — see src/lib/server/wallet/config.ts.
#
# Set certificates and keys as BASE64 of the whole PEM file, on one line:
#
#   base64 -i signerCert.pem | pbcopy
#
# Raw PEM and \n-escaped PEM are both accepted, but base64 has no newlines, no
# dashes and no spaces, so there is nothing left for a dashboard text field to
# mangle. A mangled certificate is not rejected at startup — it signs a pass that
# iOS then refuses with no error message.

# Apple. The pass type and team identifiers come from the certificate itself
# (`openssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -subject` — team ID is the OU).
#
# These certificates expire ANNUALLY. When one lapses the Apple button hides
# itself and the server logs why; reissue at developer.apple.com → Certificates
# → Pass Type ID Certificate.

APPLE_PASS_SIGNER_CERT=
APPLE_PASS_SIGNER_KEY=
APPLE_PASS_SIGNER_KEY_PASSPHRASE=
APPLE_PASS_TYPE_IDENTIFIER=
APPLE_PASS_TEAM_IDENTIFIER=

# Apple's WWDR intermediate is public and vendored at
# src/lib/server/wallet/model/wwdr.ts, so it needs no variable. This
# override exists only for the eventual G5 transition.

# APPLE_PASS_WWDR_CERT=

# Google. The service account is base64 of the ENTIRE key file JSON:
#
#   base64 -i service-account.json | pbcopy
#
# Pasting the raw multi-line JSON here does not work — dotenv stops at the first
# newline and stores a bare "{". The account needs the Wallet Object Issuer role
# on the issuer below, granted in the Google Pay & Wallet Console.
#
# Note the issuer starts in demo mode: until Google grants production access,
# only email addresses explicitly added as test accounts can save a pass, and
# everyone else gets a generic error. Request it early; it has a review queue.
#
# The class suffix is per-event, so last year's passes keep their own artwork.

GOOGLE_WALLET_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=
GOOGLE_WALLET_ISSUER_ID=
GOOGLE_WALLET_CLASS_SUFFIX=rodeo-2026

# Pass artwork (icon/logo/strip and pass.json) ships in the repo under
# src/lib/server/wallet/model/ and is used as-is locally. Point this at a
# public-read bucket or CDN to override it in production without a deploy —
# there are no secrets in these files. Any fetch failure falls back to the
# bundled copy, so a bad URL degrades the artwork and never the feature.
#
# Do NOT reuse bare AWS_* variable names on Vercel: the Node runtime sets
# AWS_REGION and the Lambda credential variables itself, and any SDK would
# silently pick up the platform's values instead of yours.

# WALLET_ASSETS_BASE_URL=https://rodeo-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/ticket.pass

# Force the asset source. Defaults to remote in production when a base URL is
# set, local otherwise. Set it to `remote` locally to exercise that path — it is
# otherwise only ever executed in production.

# WALLET_ASSETS_SOURCE=local

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# File storage (S3) — optional everywhere
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Backs file uploads. Nothing here is validated at boot: with none of it set,
# storage reports itself unconfigured and whatever would have used it simply
# isn't offered. Set all four or none — src/lib/server/s3.ts prints a warning at
# startup naming what's missing if you set some, because a single typo would
# otherwise present only as "the upload control isn't there".
#
# Do NOT name these AWS_REGION / AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY.
# Vercel's Node runtime sets those itself for the function, so an SDK left to its
# default credential chain authenticates as the platform's execution role against
# the platform's region — against the wrong bucket, without erroring. The client
# in src/lib/server/s3.ts is handed these values explicitly so that chain is never
# consulted; the S3_ prefix is what keeps the two sets from colliding.
#
# Set up a bucket of its own for this deploy:
#
#   1. Create the bucket. Leave Block Public Access fully ON — nothing is served
#      from it directly. Reads go out as short-lived presigned URLs, so the
#      bucket never needs to be public and no CORS rule is needed while uploads
#      are proxied through the app.
#   2. Create an IAM user with programmatic access and no console login, and
#      attach an inline policy scoped to this bucket alone:
#
#      {
#        "Version": "2012-10-17",
#        "Statement": [{
#          "Effect": "Allow",
#          "Action": ["s3:PutObject", "s3:GetObject", "s3:DeleteObject"],
#          "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::YOUR-BUCKET/*"
#        }]
#      }
#
#      No s3:ListBucket: nothing enumerates the bucket. The previous Rodeo used
#      ListObjectsV2 to discover wallet artwork, which this doesn't need — the
#      file names are fixed and the keys for uploads are derived from ids.
#   3. Put the access key on the deploy as the two variables below.
#
# Uploads are capped at 4 MB (MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES in src/lib/server/s3.ts). That is
# not an arbitrary choice: Vercel rejects a serverless function request body over
# 4.5 MB with a 413 before any of our code runs.

# S3_REGION=us-east-1
# S3_BUCKET=
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set automatically by Vercel — leave unset locally
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# VERCEL_ENV = production | preview | development. It, not NODE_ENV, decides
# whether real auth and real email are in play.
